##What AI Diagnosed

October 2026
Georgia Wyess

Right after I finished college I worked two jobs: one as a mathematics teacher, and one as a Program Coordinator at a local community college. Both jobs meant that I went from being a student, to being the one running a classroom without much turnaround time.

At the start of the spring semester, I was invited to a seminar for teachers and administrators that focused on AI in the classroom. I was new — both to teaching and to guiding college programs — and had gone mostly to listen. At one point the session leaders passed out sticky notes and stickers and had us write down what we thought AI’s biggest impact on students would be; we’d then walk around and stick them on poster boards with topics that matched our note, and also encouraged to place stickers next to those notes we agreed with. Unsurprisingly, the note that received the most stickers was the following: 




Both claims really bothered me but I couldn’t quite explain why. I spent a lot of time thinking about the claim that critical thinking is threatened by AI, and I finally came to the conclusion that it felt wrong to me because the sticky notes have misordered the sequence of events. The underlying assumption is that students were thinking critically before the release of publicly available LLMs and instructors are fearful that this skill is going to disappear, but the more I consider what was happening before AI entered the conversation the less I believe the former. AI never drove the decline of critical thought, it instead revealed a large flaw in education.

There are two causes that are driving the decline in critical thought and neither of them has to do with the availability of new technology. The first is that colleges have stopped being optional and instead are now seen as a minimum achievement. The second is that even before college, memorization and repetition are natural byproducts of the structure of our education, and that thinking was never a cultivated skill.

—
Before considering the role AI plays in a college, you need to ask why people are going at all. 

Not too long ago, people could buy a home, support a family, and live a good life on a blue-collar salary. You didn’t have to have a college degree. If college didn’t seem like the right path for you, it was perfectly okay to take over the family business or go to trade school, and no one thought less of you for making that choice. 

This held true even for white-collar; it was simply more open. People who didn’t have a college degree, or had completed a few community college courses were able to find work for companies such as HP, Yahoo!, Nuance, and more around the time of the dot-com boom. As my dad likes to say, the only requirement at the time was having a beating pulse. A reputable reference to a good company got you through the door. 

Employers, however, were simultaneously screening for people with degrees, especially when it came to positions of executive leadership. It’s worth considering why. If it’s not a requirement for getting a job then it must possess some other intrinsic value. I believe it’s what the degree signaled. 

You could be a particularly good employee without a college background: you could follow instruction, provide support where needed, be timely, and stay out of trouble. This does have inherent value; after all, businesses need a workforce to execute goals. But profitability doesn’t lie only in carrying out objectives, it also needs to produce growth, and growth is often driven by those people who can figure out what no one has told them. At the time, when college was still optional, the people who were college graduates were also a large proportion of the “growth” population. They attended schools because they took ideas and questioning the status quo seriously, they wanted to be in places where they were encouraged to be contrarian, and these people are also the ones that often excel in positions on leadership and direction — particularly because you need to have an inherent drive to work towards goals that no one has defined, and contrarians are quite good at this {1}.

The degree soon became a signal, and it was an unusually good one because it was too expensive to fake. For employers it didn’t matter what your degree was in, what mattered is that you had a college degree. And this is the same narrative that a lot of my teachers ingrained in me during my early education: it doesn’t matter what you study, go to college if you want to be successful. 

With a signal this powerful, it didn’t take long before people beyond the hiring-world caught on. The youth aren’t stupid. They saw that better-paying positions were made more accessible after graduating from college, and they took the logical next steps: they went. 

So people started going to college at higher rates and eventually the college degree stopped being as rare of a possession as it once was, and this meant the signal stopped meaning what it used to. Today, it’s not whether you want to go to college, it’s more of whether you can afford not to. A B.A. or a B.S. is now a minimum requirement for most well-paying jobs. Sure, blue-collar is still an option but given current economic conditions, it’s unlikely to afford you the same quality of living a white-collar job will. This necessity meant that colleges now get thousands of more applicants annually, the average price of an education goes up, acceptance rates go down, and, fundamentally, the reason for attendance shifts.

Degrees used to mean that it’s owner chose to challenge themselves for four years with hard thinking when they could have just as easily gotten a job; now it says the owner did what was required of them. These are two very different signals.

Colleges never changes much. The institutions that once valued critical thinking and being at the frontier of novelty still build their practices on these same principles. These ideals worked particularly well when those attending also wanted these ideas to work, now a majority of students, if they’re aware of these definitions at all, are apathetic towards them. People in college go for the grades and diploma that will get them the interview.

You see this reflected in the work that has been produced over the years. In conversation with some of my old professors, they admitted to me that over the course of their tenure, they’ve seen the novelty of undergraduate ideas decline. On writing assignments or research projects, the same ideas are repeated almost year-by-year, across different courses. Students are echoing back the ideas of the professors, and checking the boxes that’ll guarantee an A. And this all started before AI became available to the public.

—
This loss of novelty is what a lot of professors mean when they worry about critical thinking: student’s aren’t engaging in or challenging ideas the way they used to. Given that, I’d define critical thinking not only as evaluating ideas, but also as the ability to produce something that wasn’t handed to you. Now it’s commonplace in classrooms for students to wait for answers that they can give back on tests, and no one is coming to their own arguments that are actually interesting.

What’s underneath this is that we don’t actually teach students how to be critical thinkers at all. Not in college, and not before. Colleges used to be institutions where the natural critical thinkers would self-select for attendance so it never needed to be taught, while K-12 schools train students to absorb information and repeat memorized facts on tests. Our education teaches recall, not thought. 

This happens through the incentive system. Many students learn early on — whether it be pushed by parents, or by peers — that the goal is to get good grades, and unsurprisingly, many of them make those moves that most reliably produces favorable outcomes {3}. Here is where students learn to repeat the opinions of their instructors rather than arguing what you truly believe; or many wait for an opinion to be given to them rather than first deciding what they actually think. Teachers can emphasize the importance of critical thinking to no end, but when a student comes to a crossroads between questioning these ideas and what the grades reward, many will choose the grade. And I don’t think they’re wrong to.

Grades are a great example of where this goal dissonance occurs. The grade is meant to represent how well you understand a subject, yet it’s perceived as an indicator of their worth. To them, a B or better means they’re a “good” student. A low grade doesn’t mean “review” or try a new way of understanding, it means “I’m bad at math.” 

I’ve struggled with this personally. At one point I tried not writing a grade on student’s math tests at all and only marking what questions they missed and circling where their thinking diverged in their work. Then I started writing the number of questions they got right on the exam without assigning a letter-value or a denominator to their work. I was trying to encourage them to actually go back and look at what they got wrong rather than looking for that all-encompassing score. The number one response I got from all of my students was, “so, what grade is this?”

And I wish I could honestly tell them that the grade doesn’t matter, that I really want them to go back and review what they missed, but the truth is that they make a good point. Colleges ask for transcripts. A good GPA is the same kind of required prerequisite for getting into a good college as a degree is for getting a job; and the higher the GPA, the better the school they get into which means the better job they get. When the measurement is what brings about the reward, people begin to optimize the measurement. It’s not a character flaw, it’s a rational response. 

What you get is hollow thinking. Students are checking rubrics and collecting points. They’re pattern matching their way through math and using SparkNotes to analyze assigned reading for them rather than ever actually opening the book. The ideas they produce aren’t theirs and once the year is over, all that information is forgotten.

School has always been performance, and even top performers are just better at playing their part. It might sound like a contradiction to what I said about college being full of original thinkers before but I don’t think it really does. I suspect that college never actually taught critical thinking so much as it attracted the people who already did it, and enjoyed it. The students who selected college over employment when attendance was optional had the habit of critical thinking ingrained in them. When college became mandatory, the filter disappeared and colleges became flooded with actors playing their role.

—
Now introduce AI into the conversation. 

Students have spent over a decade learning that producing a single correct output is the goal of any assignment; the thinking isn’t important to the process {4}. When you hand someone a machine that completes this task instantly, it would be ludicrous not to use it. AI isn’t taking student’s ability to think. It’s saving them time and removing the road block in front of them that is the given assignment. The thinking that educators had believed they were cultivating through assignments and projects was never there, it was predetermined ideas rearranged into a coherent essay by someone who had become a master at doing so. AI just makes the rearranging automatic.

Outsourcing thinking has been a long standing practice. The only thing AI has done as of late is make that process instant. The apathy that many college professors are confronted with isn’t a recent phenomenon caused by new technology, it all connects back to how students were taught to “learn” and the necessity of a college education. 

A clear objection is that even if AI didn’t start this phenomenon, it definitely has proliferated it. I do agree with this, and it’s a claim I think demands attention too. Before AI, faking thinking at least took some effort by requiring students to at least read about something, and maybe they inadvertently learned a thing or two along the way. Undergraduate work is where new ideas start, and work that only draws on statistically optimized outcomes of already established information doesn’t contribute anything novel. This does mean that we can expect a stagnation at the front lines of academic thought. 

However, causing a problem and making it worse are two separate things, and this difference matters because it decides what you try to fix. If AI is the catalyst, then you ban it in schools, find ways to detect it, and design assignments around it in ways that make using it unfavorable. If AI is the accelerant, the banning it just reverts the situation to the way it was before, which means you’re defending the performative nature of schools. 

The real test is this: if AI can achieve the goal of the assignment, then the assignment was likely measuring performance rather than encouraging students to think. This is uncomfortable for a lot of teachers, especially because it means a lot of teaching styles and assignments are going to have to be redesigned, and I’m not happy about it either. It does mean, however, that AI has given us something that we didn’t have previously: a honest way to find out which assignments are actually having a positive growth effect on our student’s thinking habits.

Colleges, on the other hand, still conduct undergraduate education as if every student really attends to produce original work. As much as I would like this to be true, it just isn’t. Some students are even actively resistant to this, after years of feeling like school is something they have to do, rather than something they are doing for themselves. AI doesn’t threaten these students’ critical thinking because they really never built these skills in the first place. 

So if you want to solve the current problem, you need to begin with why AI was so appealing to students in the first place. It’s appealing because it automates those exact processes students have been practicing for at least a decade: identifying the goal of a given prompt, pattern matching it to follow the correct steps, and producing an optimized output for a high reward. That’s the exact process that AI uses to answer inputs. It would be almost questionable not to use it, especially when your real interests lie beyond the assignment. 

So how do we fix this problem? The truth is, I really don’t know. Do we give students a reason to use AI less? Do we convince them that thinking is really valuable? How do we teach them to use this new technology without temping them to give into it completely? My experiment with removing a letter grade from assignments didn’t work, but I think it aimed at the right problem. That problem was never the tool, it’s the outcome that we’ve taught students to reach for.

